  "summary": "Recent preprints and a published study test how thermal, seasonal and insecticide pressures shape adaptive divergence in Drosophila and stickleback.",
  "synthesis": "The dominant thread is the genomic basis of rapid adaptive change in ectotherms facing thermal, seasonal, chemical and predation pressures. A preprint on the pest fly Drosophila suzukii tests whether seasonal adaptive tracking, linked to thermal tolerance evolution and seasonal allele frequency shifts, contributes to invasion success. Pooled sequencing of 12 replicate experimental-evolution populations identifies genomic regions under selection in divergent chronotypes. An outdoor insecticide experiment reports that half of the populations went extinct despite strong parallel polygenic adaptation, and pre-adaptation is examined in relation to these outcomes. In Icelandic threespine stickleback adapted to geothermal warming, transgenerational and within-generation plasticity show distinct genetic architectures. A published stickleback study shows that paternal predation cues alter early gene expression in sons and daughters through distinct pathways. What is new is the separation of plasticity components and the explicit linkage of genomic trajectories to contingent evolutionary outcomes.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-haiku-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-11-23"
}
